00:13:35.790 --> 00:13:38.120
It's not just a big
warehouse full of servers.

00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:41.240
Our sweet spot is providing
solutions and ecosystems

00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:43.620
where people can actually
connect to each other.

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:45.470
So if you look at the
Cloud Edge nodes that

00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:48.710
are deployed globally out
there, about 50% of those

00:13:48.710 --> 00:13:50.040
are in Equinix data centers.

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:53.310
So the rest of the industry
splits the other 50%.

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:55.310
So what that gives
us visibility into is

00:13:55.310 --> 00:13:57.840
who's connecting to who,
what are they doing with it,

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:01.230
how are they using it, what are
the use cases they're doing?

00:14:01.230 --> 00:14:04.040
And so what we've done
is we've analyzed just

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:08.420
over about 800 different
enterprises to look at,

00:14:08.420 --> 00:14:11.510
what are the trends that we're
seeing for how people are

00:14:11.510 --> 00:14:13.490
transforming their
infrastructure

00:14:13.490 --> 00:14:15.553
to be ready for
digital transformation?

00:14:15.553 --> 00:14:16.970
And so what we've
noticed is there

00:14:16.970 --> 00:14:21.420
are four very distinct steps
that folks are going through.

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:23.732
And typically, they're
in this exact order.

00:14:23.732 --> 00:14:25.190
So if you're an
enterprise customer

00:14:25.190 --> 00:14:26.750
and you're wondering,
well, how do I

00:14:26.750 --> 00:14:30.020
enable this transformation of
making my infrastructure ready

00:14:30.020 --> 00:14:32.900
for digital transformation,
this is sort of the roadmap

00:14:32.900 --> 00:14:34.370
that we're seeing.

00:14:34.370 --> 00:14:38.798
Or if you're a vendor who's
coming up with solutions,

00:14:38.798 --> 00:14:40.340
these are the steps
that we're seeing

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:43.260
enterprises take in order to be
ready for that transformation.

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:45.380
So step one is,
typically, they'll

00:14:45.380 --> 00:14:47.750
want to optimize their network.

00:14:47.750 --> 00:14:51.350
So typically, applications will
be hosted in their basement,

00:14:51.350 --> 00:14:54.710
and then all of their users will
be using a wide area network

00:14:54.710 --> 00:14:56.510
to get to those applications.

00:14:56.510 --> 00:14:59.010
The first step is to distribute
those applications out,

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:02.660
optimize your WAN
is step number one.

00:15:02.660 --> 00:15:04.720
So take those applications--

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:08.030
get those applications
closer to the end users

00:15:08.030 --> 00:15:11.050
to reduce latency, to
actually drive down costs.

00:15:11.050 --> 00:15:12.620
So that's step one.

00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:17.780
Typically once enterprises
have optimized their WAN

00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:20.180
to distribute out
their applications,

00:15:20.180 --> 00:15:22.700
they then use those
nodes to connect out

00:15:22.700 --> 00:15:26.183
to enable a multi-hybrid
cloud architecture.

00:15:26.183 --> 00:15:28.100
So we'll double click
on that one a little bit

00:15:28.100 --> 00:15:29.180
here in a minute.

00:15:29.180 --> 00:15:31.850
And then once they have the
distributed architecture

00:15:31.850 --> 00:15:33.590
connected out to
the clouds, they

00:15:33.590 --> 00:15:37.280
fortify that with a pretty
robust security perimeter.

00:15:37.280 --> 00:15:39.020
Once that security
perimeter is there,

00:15:39.020 --> 00:15:41.590
we typically see them move
a lot more of their data

00:15:41.590 --> 00:15:43.980
closer to those applications.

00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:46.460
So that's the rough
progression of what

00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:48.920
we see when we look at
these 800 customers of how

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:51.920
they're on this journey.

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:55.430
Double clicking down into
the hybrid multi-cloud, so

00:15:55.430 --> 00:15:58.580
what do folks care
about when they're

00:15:58.580 --> 00:16:01.190
taking that second step
for hybrid multi-cloud?

00:16:01.190 --> 00:16:04.260
Number one, they care
what clouds are available.

00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:06.980
So what we see typically
is that, if a customer is

00:16:06.980 --> 00:16:09.380
connected to one cloud,
they're most likely

00:16:09.380 --> 00:16:12.080
going to connect to at
least two other clouds.

00:16:12.080 --> 00:16:14.570
So having that
cloud availability

00:16:14.570 --> 00:16:17.300
is absolutely critical.

00:16:17.300 --> 00:16:21.110
I mentioned the edge nodes
that Equinix has available.

00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:23.330
GCI is available in
Equinix locations

00:16:23.330 --> 00:16:28.020
in 22 different metros,
so that's important.

00:16:28.020 --> 00:16:30.682
The second big step
is performance.

00:16:30.682 --> 00:16:32.390
So you want to make
sure that when you're

00:16:32.390 --> 00:16:33.800
deploying your
infrastructure, it's

00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:35.510
in a place that's
going to have the best

00:16:35.510 --> 00:16:37.610
performance for
connecting into the cloud

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:41.480
that you care about, as well as
provides the option to scale,

00:16:41.480 --> 00:16:43.370
both from a performance
standpoint and a cost

00:16:43.370 --> 00:16:44.240
standpoint.

00:16:44.240 --> 00:16:46.250
So a pattern that we see
from a lot of customers

00:16:46.250 --> 00:16:49.233
is they will start
on Cloud Exchange.

00:16:49.233 --> 00:16:50.900
So Cloud Exchange is
a platform that you

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:54.410
can set up virtual connections
to multiple clouds.

00:16:54.410 --> 00:16:57.440
But once that starts to scale
to 10 gig or multi 10 gig

00:16:57.440 --> 00:17:00.080
connections, having the
option to pull that off

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:02.810
to a cross connect is a
really important capability

00:17:02.810 --> 00:17:07.020
to have, both for performance as
well as for a cost standpoint.

00:17:07.020 --> 00:17:09.020
So pulling that over to
a cross connect and have

00:17:09.020 --> 00:17:11.990
that option available is
really important to customers.

00:17:11.990 --> 00:17:17.500
And there nine of the 11
regions of the GCI regions

00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:19.641
are available in Equinix today.

00:17:19.641 --> 00:17:21.099
I think the next
closest competitor

00:17:21.099 --> 00:17:24.710
has two locations available.

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:26.470
And then the third
option, of course,

00:17:26.470 --> 00:17:28.420
is ease of use and
flexibility, and that

00:17:28.420 --> 00:17:32.020
means both technical ease
of use and flexibility,

00:17:32.020 --> 00:17:33.520
as well as commercial
flexibility.

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:35.770
So being able to
set up connections.

00:17:35.770 --> 00:17:38.380
Obviously when you're
using multi-cloud,

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:40.900
being able to connect
into and out of,

00:17:40.900 --> 00:17:43.180
move workloads
around dynamically.

00:17:43.180 --> 00:17:45.990
You can set up connections,
tear those connections down

00:17:45.990 --> 00:17:49.280
in a very flexible way,
is super important.

00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:53.020
So a little bit of a
double click on that.

00:17:53.020 --> 00:17:56.590
Through Cloud Exchange, you can
set up connections, tear them

00:17:56.590 --> 00:17:59.330
down, to any location
anywhere in the world.

00:17:59.330 --> 00:18:02.710
So if you're in Sydney and
you have a workload that

00:18:02.710 --> 00:18:06.310
needs to access Google Cloud
in Ashburn or somewhere,

00:18:06.310 --> 00:18:09.920
you can use Cloud Exchange to
set that up, or for redundancy.

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:11.920
But again, you can set
that up, tear those down,

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:15.130
in a very flexible architecture.

00:18:15.130 --> 00:18:18.040
Two quick use cases
that we're seeing.

00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:23.290
The first and probably by
far the predominant use

00:18:23.290 --> 00:18:26.680
case that we're
seeing is connecting

00:18:26.680 --> 00:18:30.430
on premise or within the
enterprise applications

00:18:30.430 --> 00:18:32.000
out to Cloud assets.

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:35.290
So being able to have the
infrastructure that you have

00:18:35.290 --> 00:18:37.690
in the cloud appear on
your corporate network

00:18:37.690 --> 00:18:39.910
such that you can
have applications

00:18:39.910 --> 00:18:41.980
in your own private
enterprise WAN

00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:44.320
and be able to access
at those assets

00:18:44.320 --> 00:18:47.110
and have them appear on their
same private network, that's

00:18:47.110 --> 00:18:49.150
the prime use case number one.

00:18:49.150 --> 00:18:50.950
Use case number two is--

00:18:50.950 --> 00:18:53.350
we're actually in
limited availability

00:18:53.350 --> 00:18:54.880
with this capability now.

00:18:54.880 --> 00:18:56.470
We're going to be
rolling it out--

00:18:56.470 --> 00:19:01.780
where you can spin up to be able
to do multi-cloud use cases.

00:19:01.780 --> 00:19:04.990
So whether you have a data
set that you want to move out

00:19:04.990 --> 00:19:08.440
of AWS into GCI, or if
it's an application where

00:19:08.440 --> 00:19:10.120
you want to have
compute in one cloud

00:19:10.120 --> 00:19:12.550
and you want to have
analytics in another cloud,

00:19:12.550 --> 00:19:15.130
being able to set
up a virtual router

00:19:15.130 --> 00:19:18.040
and move data into and
out of multiple clouds

00:19:18.040 --> 00:19:19.420
is also a second use case.

00:19:19.420 --> 00:19:21.310
And you can go to
our booth and we

00:19:21.310 --> 00:19:23.100
can show you a demo
of exactly what that's

00:19:23.100 --> 00:19:26.210
going to look like as well.

00:19:26.210 --> 00:19:27.905
So with that, this
is information

00:19:27.905 --> 00:19:29.530
about how to come
and find us, and I'll

00:19:29.530 --> 00:19:31.990
be around after the session for
a couple of minutes as well.

00:19:31.990 --> 00:19:32.615
Thank you guys.

00:19:38.188 --> 00:19:39.480
MATT SIMPSON: OK, good morning.

00:19:42.780 --> 00:19:44.370
All right.

00:19:44.370 --> 00:19:48.190
I promise it's going to be close
to a blockbuster hit today.

00:19:48.190 --> 00:19:51.310
But again, I think
we need the popcorn.

00:19:51.310 --> 00:19:55.170
But I'm going to talk a
little bit about Megaport.

00:19:55.170 --> 00:19:58.800
Our vision is to
simplify connectivity.

00:19:58.800 --> 00:20:00.000
When you're looking at--

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:03.240
I guess Rohith was mentioning
before, multi-cloud,

00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:04.830
and also Bill--

00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:06.480
it can be very complex.

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:08.910
And I guess our
vision is that we

00:20:08.910 --> 00:20:11.100
want to ensure that
you are focused

00:20:11.100 --> 00:20:14.310
on getting the best performance
out of your applications.

00:20:14.310 --> 00:20:17.830
And so our vision is to simplify
that connectivity and abstract

00:20:17.830 --> 00:20:21.360
some of the complexity out of
getting connected to cloud.

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:24.730
But it's not only getting
connected to cloud,

00:20:24.730 --> 00:20:27.210
and I think Bill
touched on this as well.

00:20:27.210 --> 00:20:31.440
It's a rich, deep
ecosystem out there to get,

00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:35.430
of course, full,
end to end solution.

00:20:35.430 --> 00:20:38.610
And Megaport, of course,
is about connecting

00:20:38.610 --> 00:20:42.420
not only the data centers but
the network providers, managed

00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:46.620
service providers, and a
full ecosystem out there.

00:20:46.620 --> 00:20:50.670
We have 1350 customers
that connect today

00:20:50.670 --> 00:20:57.750
across 425 data centers,
which 82 of those

00:20:57.750 --> 00:20:59.400
are unique data
center operators,

00:20:59.400 --> 00:21:03.810
so we do have Equinix
and CyrusOne and Cyxtera

00:21:03.810 --> 00:21:07.500
and Interxion and Flexential,
and Digital Realty, of course.

00:21:07.500 --> 00:21:12.360
So what's unique
about our offering is

00:21:12.360 --> 00:21:15.510
that we believe that
it should be fast,

00:21:15.510 --> 00:21:19.230
it should be with ease,
and you should have choice,

00:21:19.230 --> 00:21:22.020
because at the end
of the day, you

00:21:22.020 --> 00:21:25.970
need to be focused on
those applications.

00:21:25.970 --> 00:21:29.360
So we touched on over
the last five years,

00:21:29.360 --> 00:21:31.520
Megaport has been
building out layer

00:21:31.520 --> 00:21:36.140
two, a software-defined
network, an open and neutral

00:21:36.140 --> 00:21:37.963
interconnection platform.

00:21:37.963 --> 00:21:39.380
And over the last
five years we've

00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:44.030
been building out to those data
centers a redundant network

00:21:44.030 --> 00:21:46.640
across all of those
facilities, and generally

00:21:46.640 --> 00:21:48.720
that's where those
service providers live,

00:21:48.720 --> 00:21:51.350
all those
interconnection points.

00:21:51.350 --> 00:21:55.860
And so over the past
five years, our customers

00:21:55.860 --> 00:21:58.190
have been connecting
at layer two.

00:21:58.190 --> 00:22:00.440
But what we have been
seeing is customers

00:22:00.440 --> 00:22:03.920
are wanting that cloud
to cloud connectivity,

00:22:03.920 --> 00:22:06.380
and whilst they might
have infrastructure,

00:22:06.380 --> 00:22:08.840
that hybrid scenario
with layer two,

00:22:08.840 --> 00:22:10.820
they're also looking
at connecting cloud

00:22:10.820 --> 00:22:14.360
to cloud without going
and procuring network

00:22:14.360 --> 00:22:17.960
hardware and space and power.

00:22:17.960 --> 00:22:23.660
So across the 425, you can
establish a service or a port

00:22:23.660 --> 00:22:29.190
with our particular service
anywhere across the globe.

00:22:29.190 --> 00:22:31.190
It takes a couple of
minutes to provision.

00:22:31.190 --> 00:22:36.098
And you can also scale that
bandwidth in real time.

00:22:36.098 --> 00:22:38.390
So I'm going to touch a little
bit about Megaport Cloud

00:22:38.390 --> 00:22:39.320
Router.

00:22:39.320 --> 00:22:41.610
It's a solution
that our customers

00:22:41.610 --> 00:22:45.680
were coming to us about,
and it was, I have my data.

00:22:45.680 --> 00:22:48.530
I've got my on-prem and
I'm connecting to cloud.

00:22:48.530 --> 00:22:51.740
But now I want to look at
connecting the two cloud

00:22:51.740 --> 00:22:52.380
endpoints.

00:22:52.380 --> 00:22:56.540
So in this scenario, it's a
private, layer three service,

00:22:56.540 --> 00:22:59.750
and you can establish this at
a one gig, two and 1/2 gig,

00:22:59.750 --> 00:23:02.280
or even 10 gig if you like.

00:23:02.280 --> 00:23:07.200
And this is a managed layer
three connectivity service.

00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:09.860
So what we have done
with this virtual router

00:23:09.860 --> 00:23:13.370
is abstracted some of
that configuration,

00:23:13.370 --> 00:23:18.110
and it also allows you to,
if you don't have your own AS

00:23:18.110 --> 00:23:21.470
or your own IP, then
this device will actually

00:23:21.470 --> 00:23:23.375
manage that for you and
automatically assign.

00:23:26.700 --> 00:23:30.330
So in a typical scenario
a customer may come to us

00:23:30.330 --> 00:23:32.580
and say, OK, I want to have
a little bit of mixture

00:23:32.580 --> 00:23:37.080
of on-prem and then a cloud,
with a hybrid scenario,

00:23:37.080 --> 00:23:39.870
and then they'll also
look at cloud to cloud.

00:23:39.870 --> 00:23:43.050
So I'm using this diagram
as a bit of an overview

00:23:43.050 --> 00:23:45.150
of a virtual router.

00:23:45.150 --> 00:23:48.540
I've stood it up in
a particular zone,

00:23:48.540 --> 00:23:52.770
and then I've got that variety
of multiple cloud services

00:23:52.770 --> 00:23:53.680
there.

00:23:53.680 --> 00:23:59.010
So in this case, we do provide
the service across both GCP,

00:23:59.010 --> 00:24:03.990
across 23 of the interconnect
points across the globe,

00:24:03.990 --> 00:24:05.725
and we also provide
direct peering,

00:24:05.725 --> 00:24:07.350
which is the layer
three service, which

00:24:07.350 --> 00:24:10.950
gives you access to Google's
public resources as well.

00:24:15.270 --> 00:24:17.040
And so I wanted to
share with you--

00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:21.570
it's not working that well
on this particular screen.

00:24:21.570 --> 00:24:25.870
But a typical customer
scenario might have--

00:24:25.870 --> 00:24:27.780
media and entertainment
is a great one.

00:24:27.780 --> 00:24:29.370
For animation and
rendering, they're

00:24:29.370 --> 00:24:32.010
looking for burstable traffic.

00:24:32.010 --> 00:24:36.630
It's seasonal, and they may
have their infrastructure in LA.

00:24:36.630 --> 00:24:39.240
And they'll establish a
layer two port with us,

00:24:39.240 --> 00:24:41.370
and they'll connect to
multiple different services

00:24:41.370 --> 00:24:44.550
within the West Coast of the US.

00:24:44.550 --> 00:24:48.600
But what if they had their
application in the east coast

00:24:48.600 --> 00:24:52.110
and they wanted to stand that
up pretty quickly and storage

00:24:52.110 --> 00:24:54.390
and compute might be
an AWS, but they're

00:24:54.390 --> 00:24:58.350
looking to get access to
some of the analytics on GCP.

00:24:58.350 --> 00:25:01.560
With this Megaport
cloud router, you

00:25:01.560 --> 00:25:05.940
can spin up the virtual device
within a couple of minutes

00:25:05.940 --> 00:25:07.560
and have virtual
cross connections

00:25:07.560 --> 00:25:09.720
between GCP and AWS.

00:25:09.720 --> 00:25:11.730
So, a private, layer
three connection.

00:25:11.730 --> 00:25:14.200
It doesn't touch
the public internet.

00:25:14.200 --> 00:25:16.080
And then if you want
to, you can actually

00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:19.560
connect those to our
services back to West Coast

00:25:19.560 --> 00:25:24.240
if you want to for
a one-off migration.

00:25:24.240 --> 00:25:26.160
In that particular
scenario, then you

00:25:26.160 --> 00:25:29.280
can turn that device off
after you've used it,

00:25:29.280 --> 00:25:33.930
whether it be a month, a day,
or a year, or even an hour.

00:25:33.930 --> 00:25:34.860
The choice is yours.

00:25:38.650 --> 00:25:42.450
We talked a little bit about
the application and seasonal

00:25:42.450 --> 00:25:47.220
and bursting of traffic, and
what we've done with Megaport

00:25:47.220 --> 00:25:51.570
is we've aligned our business
model similar to cloud.

00:25:51.570 --> 00:25:56.970
So you can actually spin
up your private connection

00:25:56.970 --> 00:25:59.940
from one megabit right
up to 100 gigabits

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:04.030
if you like, and you can
actually do that on demand.

00:26:04.030 --> 00:26:08.220
And in this scenario, with our
particularly seasonal traffic,

00:26:08.220 --> 00:26:12.240
you can manipulate those
speeds whenever you need to,

00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:14.820
whether it be on a weekend,
whether it be on an evening,

00:26:14.820 --> 00:26:20.100
and we'll only bill you for
what you actually consume.

00:26:20.100 --> 00:26:22.320
We've got a couple of
customers, particularly media

00:26:22.320 --> 00:26:23.970
and entertainment,
that are actually

00:26:23.970 --> 00:26:26.790
chasing some of
that cheap storage

00:26:26.790 --> 00:26:29.070
and compute, particularly
whatever provider

00:26:29.070 --> 00:26:30.310
that might be.

00:26:30.310 --> 00:26:34.290
We've had scenarios where a
customer might go from Toronto

00:26:34.290 --> 00:26:37.620
right down to Ashburn, and with
a virtual cross connection,

00:26:37.620 --> 00:26:39.780
they could do that for a month.

00:26:39.780 --> 00:26:42.180
And what's really important is--

00:26:42.180 --> 00:26:46.090
Thomas talked about an
open ecosystem this week,

00:26:46.090 --> 00:26:48.990
and with Megaport, we've
got that neutral platform

00:26:48.990 --> 00:26:52.020
that you can connect
to multiple services

00:26:52.020 --> 00:26:57.240
across 425 data centers,
across 120 cloud on-ramps,

00:26:57.240 --> 00:27:00.790
across multiple cloud providers.

00:27:00.790 --> 00:27:03.600
And it's really important
that, with Google, there's

00:27:03.600 --> 00:27:04.950
58 points of presence.

00:27:04.950 --> 00:27:07.740
They've got one of
the largest networks.

00:27:07.740 --> 00:27:10.020
But with Megaport,
you're actually

00:27:10.020 --> 00:27:14.080
connecting from 425
points of those locations.

00:27:14.080 --> 00:27:15.780
So we're extending
the reach of GCP

00:27:15.780 --> 00:27:19.440
and bringing it to the doorstep
to you as the enterprise user.

00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:23.520
And that's really important
from a performance

00:27:23.520 --> 00:27:28.050
and getting the best of
out of your application.

00:27:28.050 --> 00:27:29.380
I think that's pretty much it.

00:27:29.380 --> 00:27:32.910
So of course, like everyone
here, we've got a booth.

00:27:32.910 --> 00:27:36.360
I'll be here for the
rest of the morning,

00:27:36.360 --> 00:27:39.252
and I look forward to
discussing it with you.

00:27:39.252 --> 00:27:39.960
Thanks very much.

00:27:54.730 --> 00:27:57.040
MANAV MISHRA: Let me
get this thing straight.

00:27:57.040 --> 00:27:58.060
Hi.

00:27:58.060 --> 00:28:00.760
This is Manav.

00:28:00.760 --> 00:28:02.770
I work for Silver Peak.

00:28:02.770 --> 00:28:06.310
I run Product Management
for Silver Peak.

00:28:06.310 --> 00:28:08.300
For those of you who
don't know Silver Peak,

00:28:08.300 --> 00:28:11.830
Silver Peak is a company
that has been around

00:28:11.830 --> 00:28:13.840
for about a decade or so.

00:28:13.840 --> 00:28:17.710
We focused earlier on
on run optimization,

00:28:17.710 --> 00:28:24.220
and a few years ago, we were
one of the early vendors,

00:28:24.220 --> 00:28:26.230
early products in
the space that's

00:28:26.230 --> 00:28:30.510
now known as software-defined
wide area networks.

00:28:30.510 --> 00:28:35.570
So why are we different from
other products and services

00:28:35.570 --> 00:28:36.920
in the space?

00:28:36.920 --> 00:28:38.840
Part of the reason
is we have always

00:28:38.840 --> 00:28:42.560
taken a business intent driven
and apps driven approach

00:28:42.560 --> 00:28:44.000
to networking.

00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:46.330
So essentially what
we realized was

00:28:46.330 --> 00:28:50.420
enterprises, small
businesses, medium businesses,

00:28:50.420 --> 00:28:53.990
customers, cared more about
taking their business intent

00:28:53.990 --> 00:28:56.810
and business policies
and transforming them

00:28:56.810 --> 00:29:01.490
into network configurations,
network policies, firewall

00:29:01.490 --> 00:29:07.070
rules, routing
policies, and whatnot.

00:29:07.070 --> 00:29:10.040
And what we provide is
an abstraction to all

00:29:10.040 --> 00:29:15.065
of that, a simple abstraction
that can take a human readable,

00:29:15.065 --> 00:29:19.760
a human prescribable policy
for the treatment an enterprise

00:29:19.760 --> 00:29:22.130
admin wants the
application or the service

00:29:22.130 --> 00:29:27.020
to have, and get that translated
all the way down to the router,

00:29:27.020 --> 00:29:31.040
to the data plane, in terms
of actionable policies.

00:29:31.040 --> 00:29:35.460
What we achieve by doing that
is the quality of experience.

00:29:35.460 --> 00:29:38.000
So as we know, as the
world is progressively

00:29:38.000 --> 00:29:41.240
moving towards SaaS
and SaaS applications,

00:29:41.240 --> 00:29:44.360
the quality of performance
of those applications

00:29:44.360 --> 00:29:49.070
is largely dictated by the
ability of the networking

00:29:49.070 --> 00:29:52.160
infrastructure to know when
to intelligently break out

00:29:52.160 --> 00:29:57.140
the traffic, which public
cloud presence to send

00:29:57.140 --> 00:30:02.960
the traffic to, and to afford
the right performance for audio

00:30:02.960 --> 00:30:06.150
and video over internet.

00:30:06.150 --> 00:30:08.180
Our solution is also
built on the notion

00:30:08.180 --> 00:30:12.130
of continuous adaptation, and
what I mean by that is it's

00:30:12.130 --> 00:30:14.970
a self-learning and a
self-driving network.

00:30:14.970 --> 00:30:19.580
So looking at the data that
flows into the customer's

00:30:19.580 --> 00:30:23.090
network, we automate a
bunch of different things

00:30:23.090 --> 00:30:25.280
on behalf of the customer.

00:30:25.280 --> 00:30:28.910
We develop the learning
of packet identification,

00:30:28.910 --> 00:30:31.940
application identification,
and we have this technology

00:30:31.940 --> 00:30:35.450
called First Packet IQ
Technology, which can,

00:30:35.450 --> 00:30:38.690
looking at the very first packet
over a new application type,

00:30:38.690 --> 00:30:41.330
it can detect what the
application is, and then

00:30:41.330 --> 00:30:44.600
decide, based on the
identification, what

00:30:44.600 --> 00:30:46.280
corresponding policy
to apply to it,

00:30:46.280 --> 00:30:48.470
whether that application
or that packet

00:30:48.470 --> 00:30:52.250
needs to be back-hauled all the
way back to HQ or to the data

00:30:52.250 --> 00:30:55.730
center, or it needs
to break out locally.

00:30:55.730 --> 00:30:58.250
And we are continuously
learning it at every node,

00:30:58.250 --> 00:31:01.310
at every router in
the network, and we

00:31:01.310 --> 00:31:05.180
use that to make the network
more intelligent and more

00:31:05.180 --> 00:31:06.960
adaptive.

00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:10.650
Finally, what we also realized
working with our customers,

00:31:10.650 --> 00:31:13.355
is that no two
customers are alike.

00:31:13.355 --> 00:31:14.730
There are some
customers who want

00:31:14.730 --> 00:31:18.460
to overhaul their entire
existing infrastructure.

00:31:18.460 --> 00:31:22.260
They want to take what they have
put in place over the last 10

00:31:22.260 --> 00:31:25.140
years and move to this new world
of software-defined wide area

00:31:25.140 --> 00:31:26.160
networks.

00:31:26.160 --> 00:31:28.920
And then there are
customers who actually

00:31:28.920 --> 00:31:31.380
want an SD-WAN solution
that will play nicely,

00:31:31.380 --> 00:31:34.000
that will fit in with
the existing network,

00:31:34.000 --> 00:31:36.690
so we call it Greenfield
or Brownfield deployments.

00:31:36.690 --> 00:31:38.850
And for Brownfield
deployments, we

00:31:38.850 --> 00:31:42.990
need to have a solution that
supports a lot of the existing

00:31:42.990 --> 00:31:44.850
protocol infrastructure
that customers

00:31:44.850 --> 00:31:47.940
have in place, so
things like BGP, OSPF,

00:31:47.940 --> 00:31:51.630
things like multicast virtual
routing and forwarding

00:31:51.630 --> 00:31:52.960
capabilities.

00:31:52.960 --> 00:31:54.720
So we have a unified
solution that

00:31:54.720 --> 00:31:57.870
brings in the best of
software-defined wide area

00:31:57.870 --> 00:32:02.250
networks and mixes it with
existing infrastructure

00:32:02.250 --> 00:32:05.190
so that any type of
customer can use our product

00:32:05.190 --> 00:32:08.740
and be successful.

00:32:08.740 --> 00:32:10.650
Our success has been
recognized by Gartner,

00:32:10.650 --> 00:32:14.400
so Gartner has
started publishing

00:32:14.400 --> 00:32:19.820
what they call the Magic
Quadrant for the WAN Edge.

00:32:19.820 --> 00:32:23.160
Every technology that we call
by a particular name, Gartner

00:32:23.160 --> 00:32:24.600
has its own name for it.

00:32:24.600 --> 00:32:26.650
So software-defined
wide area networks

00:32:26.650 --> 00:32:28.560
is called WAN Edge by Gartner.

00:32:28.560 --> 00:32:31.440
We are one of the three
leaders in the Magic Quadrant.

00:32:34.200 --> 00:32:36.180
I want to talk a little
bit about our platform,

00:32:36.180 --> 00:32:39.360
and then talk about how we
enable successful Google Cloud

00:32:39.360 --> 00:32:40.690
customers.

00:32:40.690 --> 00:32:47.550
Our platform, essentially, is an
all unified, Cloud-first stack

00:32:47.550 --> 00:32:50.320
which has application
visibility and control.

00:32:50.320 --> 00:32:52.740
This is the packet
detection, identification,

00:32:52.740 --> 00:32:54.930
intelligent breakout
that I talked about.

00:32:54.930 --> 00:32:59.340
It has routing capabilities, so
essentially interplaying nicely

00:32:59.340 --> 00:33:03.480
with OSPF, BGP,
Multicast, VRF, name it.

00:33:03.480 --> 00:33:07.020
It has WAN optimization,
so our company's roots

00:33:07.020 --> 00:33:09.610
were in WAN optimization,
so WAN optimization

00:33:09.610 --> 00:33:11.770
is a built-in,
integrated feature.

00:33:11.770 --> 00:33:14.970
So for customers who want
WAN op, and there are still

00:33:14.970 --> 00:33:17.670
a lot of customers who have
their own private cloud

00:33:17.670 --> 00:33:20.760
offerings who need
WAN optimization,

00:33:20.760 --> 00:33:23.190
it's easy to enable
using our product.

00:33:23.190 --> 00:33:25.030
And network security.

00:33:25.030 --> 00:33:27.750
So we have built-in firewalls
and built-in segmentation

00:33:27.750 --> 00:33:30.180
that customers can
use so that they

00:33:30.180 --> 00:33:33.130
don't need to have
yet another appliance

00:33:33.130 --> 00:33:36.030
to afford these capabilities
for their network.

00:33:36.030 --> 00:33:38.850
And all of these
are built into what

00:33:38.850 --> 00:33:40.860
we call the EdgeConnect
Appliance, the Unity

00:33:40.860 --> 00:33:42.510
EdgeConnect Appliance.

00:33:42.510 --> 00:33:48.760
Now, the software is available
in a virtualized form,

00:33:48.760 --> 00:33:52.380
so if you want to run it
on-prem or on a hypervisor,

00:33:52.380 --> 00:33:53.610
you could do so.

00:33:53.610 --> 00:33:54.870
It's available in the cloud.

00:33:54.870 --> 00:33:56.640
We are the only
product out there

00:33:56.640 --> 00:33:59.330
which is available on
all major public clouds.

00:33:59.330 --> 00:34:02.370
So you can actually go
to Google Marketplace

00:34:02.370 --> 00:34:05.100
as your marketplace,
AWS marketplace, Oracle

00:34:05.100 --> 00:34:09.000
marketplace, and you can
easily get it and deploy it

00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:11.520
in a VPC of your choice.

00:34:11.520 --> 00:34:15.100
And it also comes with
Silver Peak hardware.

00:34:15.100 --> 00:34:17.010
So for your branch
offices where you

00:34:17.010 --> 00:34:21.540
want a local point of presence
for the software in a branch

00:34:21.540 --> 00:34:24.960
office, you can actually get
a hardware and software combo

00:34:24.960 --> 00:34:27.230
from Silver Peak.

00:34:27.230 --> 00:34:29.210
So this is essentially
our data plane.

00:34:29.210 --> 00:34:31.800
This is where the
magic actually happens.

00:34:31.800 --> 00:34:35.440
But how does the data
plane know what to do,

00:34:35.440 --> 00:34:38.960
and how does the data plane
know what policies are

00:34:38.960 --> 00:34:40.280
being configured by the user?

00:34:40.280 --> 00:34:43.230
And that happens through what
we call the orchestration plane.

00:34:43.230 --> 00:34:46.909
So we have a centralized,
business-driven orchestration

00:34:46.909 --> 00:34:48.270
solution.

00:34:48.270 --> 00:34:50.389
I think it's available
in three different forms.

00:34:50.389 --> 00:34:52.370
It's available in a SaaS form.

00:34:52.370 --> 00:34:54.750
We call it Orchestrator
as a service,

00:34:54.750 --> 00:34:58.100
so we run it in our
public cloud presence.

00:34:58.100 --> 00:35:01.400
And as a customer, think of
it just like a SaaS product.

00:35:01.400 --> 00:35:04.490
You can log into the
portal, you get access

00:35:04.490 --> 00:35:07.130
to your orchestration
for your network,

00:35:07.130 --> 00:35:10.400
and you can manage and configure
your network from there.

00:35:10.400 --> 00:35:12.410
It's also available
on-prem, so you

00:35:12.410 --> 00:35:16.280
can actually, if you have your
own on-prem infrastructure,

00:35:16.280 --> 00:35:18.650
all you need to do is
have a hypervisor layer

00:35:18.650 --> 00:35:23.300
and deploy this OVA,
or the hypervisor

00:35:23.300 --> 00:35:28.370
format of your choice, onto
your existing infrastructure.

00:35:28.370 --> 00:35:31.760
Or just like with the
virtual appliance,

00:35:31.760 --> 00:35:36.720
you can run it in a VPC in any
of the major public clouds.

00:35:36.720 --> 00:35:43.150
So you can run it on Google,
Amazon, Microsoft, Oracle.

00:35:43.150 --> 00:35:45.530
So this is sort of
the net net of what

00:35:45.530 --> 00:35:48.350
our overall product looks like.

00:35:51.770 --> 00:35:55.640
We also have an integrated
service training platform

00:35:55.640 --> 00:35:58.670
built into the product
by which, if you

00:35:58.670 --> 00:36:02.780
want to intelligently break out
traffic to a security service

00:36:02.780 --> 00:36:06.680
provider, like Zscaler or a
PaloAlto or a Checkpoint--

00:36:06.680 --> 00:36:08.680
I'm going to cover
in a subsequent slide

00:36:08.680 --> 00:36:11.300
a list of our partners--
you could do so.

00:36:11.300 --> 00:36:15.660
And the orchestration
to Zscaler is automated.

00:36:15.660 --> 00:36:18.720
So essentially all you
need to do is, in the UI,

00:36:18.720 --> 00:36:20.330
you just need to
define the policy.

00:36:20.330 --> 00:36:23.330
For traffic you could
say, hey, all the traffic

00:36:23.330 --> 00:36:26.420
that's going to my
corporate websites,

00:36:26.420 --> 00:36:28.310
send it back to the data
center, and anything

00:36:28.310 --> 00:36:30.800
that's going out to the
internet, send it to Zscaler.

00:36:30.800 --> 00:36:33.620
And then behind the scenes,
we work with Zscaler,

00:36:33.620 --> 00:36:36.440
we find out the local points
of presence for Zscaler

00:36:36.440 --> 00:36:38.990
that are closer to
that branch office,

00:36:38.990 --> 00:36:43.220
and we automate the tunnel
creation between all sites

00:36:43.220 --> 00:36:45.620
and the corresponding
Zscaler points of presence

00:36:45.620 --> 00:36:49.630
worldwide for the customer
with just one click.

00:36:49.630 --> 00:36:53.050
We also have flow
through operations,

00:36:53.050 --> 00:36:56.350
so essentially everything
that we do in the product

00:36:56.350 --> 00:36:58.430
is available by a Rest APIs.

00:36:58.430 --> 00:37:01.860
So it's a cloud-first approach
to building the product.

00:37:01.860 --> 00:37:05.530
So if you want to have access
to the detailed statistics

00:37:05.530 --> 00:37:08.890
and analytics and
logs that we have,

00:37:08.890 --> 00:37:10.990
we make it available
to our customers.

00:37:10.990 --> 00:37:13.840
And we have customers
using that and syncing it

00:37:13.840 --> 00:37:19.030
into their own data analytics
systems and data warehousing

00:37:19.030 --> 00:37:20.140
systems.

00:37:20.140 --> 00:37:22.840
We have a Splunk connector
for a bunch of our enterprise

00:37:22.840 --> 00:37:24.570
customers who use it.

00:37:24.570 --> 00:37:27.760
They import the data from
our systems into Splunk

00:37:27.760 --> 00:37:30.490
and then they do their own
detailed analysis and insight

00:37:30.490 --> 00:37:31.360
gathering from that.

00:37:34.010 --> 00:37:35.448
The Unity EdgeConnect
Appliance--

00:37:35.448 --> 00:37:36.990
I'm just going to
quickly cover this.

00:37:36.990 --> 00:37:39.260
As I said, it's available
in a physical, virtual, and

00:37:39.260 --> 00:37:40.200
cloud-based form.

00:37:40.200 --> 00:37:45.560
It runs Unity Boost, which is
our WAN optimization feature.

00:37:45.560 --> 00:37:50.310
It has fast packet detection.

00:37:50.310 --> 00:37:52.320
The one thing I wanted
to touch over here

00:37:52.320 --> 00:37:55.100
is we have zero touch
branch deployments,

00:37:55.100 --> 00:37:58.650
so we have zero touch
provisioning and zero touch

00:37:58.650 --> 00:38:00.040
configuration.

00:38:00.040 --> 00:38:03.090
So essentially, if
you're an IT admin

00:38:03.090 --> 00:38:07.350
and you want to send a
replacement box into a branch

00:38:07.350 --> 00:38:10.110
office in your
organization but you

00:38:10.110 --> 00:38:12.750
want to preconfigure
all the policies for it,

00:38:12.750 --> 00:38:15.990
you could actually just use
our scripts, use our Rest APIs.

00:38:15.990 --> 00:38:19.277
We configure the box, and when
the branch office gets the box,

00:38:19.277 --> 00:38:21.610
all they have to do is open
it up and plug in the cables

00:38:21.610 --> 00:38:23.340
and it's good to go.

00:38:23.340 --> 00:38:26.210
Or you can use zero
touch provisioning,

00:38:26.210 --> 00:38:29.240
which is where we ship a new
box to the branch office,

00:38:29.240 --> 00:38:32.520
the branch office connects
the box to cables,

00:38:32.520 --> 00:38:35.760
goes into the Orchestrator,
Orchestrator shows

00:38:35.760 --> 00:38:38.670
that a new device or a new
appliance has been detected,

00:38:38.670 --> 00:38:41.060
and then you let the
orchestrator orchestrate it

00:38:41.060 --> 00:38:42.130
on your behalf.

00:38:42.130 --> 00:38:45.738
So essentially think of it
as the modern Pixel or iPhone

00:38:45.738 --> 00:38:47.280
experience where
you just have to log

00:38:47.280 --> 00:38:51.510
in with your username and
ID, and the policies and data

00:38:51.510 --> 00:38:53.963
that you have for that
particular device automatically

00:38:53.963 --> 00:38:55.380
are provisioned
through the cloud.

00:38:58.020 --> 00:39:00.460
So this is a list
of our partners.

00:39:00.460 --> 00:39:03.520
As I said, we work
with a variety of them.

00:39:03.520 --> 00:39:07.430
PaloAlto, Zscaler, CheckPoint,
Forcepoint, Symantec.

00:39:07.430 --> 00:39:10.590
The list is endless.

00:39:10.590 --> 00:39:15.030
Coming on to how we enable
success for Google and Google

00:39:15.030 --> 00:39:15.930
Cloud customers.

00:39:15.930 --> 00:39:18.090
As I mentioned, our
virtual appliance

00:39:18.090 --> 00:39:20.130
is available on
Google Cloud today.

00:39:20.130 --> 00:39:21.720
You could give it a try.

00:39:24.690 --> 00:39:29.850
We have quite a few
customers who have deployed

00:39:29.850 --> 00:39:32.940
and are using Silver Peak
on Google Cloud today,

00:39:32.940 --> 00:39:36.090
and they are using Silver
Peak on Google Cloud in two

00:39:36.090 --> 00:39:38.140
different deployment models.

00:39:38.140 --> 00:39:40.380
So we have customers who
are new in their journey

00:39:40.380 --> 00:39:44.310
to Google Cloud,
or who basically

00:39:44.310 --> 00:39:48.190
want to enable a cloud on-ramp
for a particular organization

00:39:48.190 --> 00:39:50.730
within their enterprise.

00:39:50.730 --> 00:39:54.660
So they basically create a
single VPC on Google Cloud

00:39:54.660 --> 00:39:58.660
and then deploy our EdgeConnect
appliance onto that.

00:39:58.660 --> 00:40:01.980
And then we also have more
complicated and complex

00:40:01.980 --> 00:40:05.220
deployments where customers
have a large presence on Google

00:40:05.220 --> 00:40:08.810
Cloud and they have a bunch
of different apps, services,

00:40:08.810 --> 00:40:11.190
data sets residing
on Google Cloud

00:40:11.190 --> 00:40:13.710
and they want access
to all of these

00:40:13.710 --> 00:40:15.330
to the rest of the enterprise.

00:40:15.330 --> 00:40:19.550
And what they do is they deploy
Silver Peak on Google Cloud,

00:40:19.550 --> 00:40:22.590
use a load balancer,
and use that to serve

00:40:22.590 --> 00:40:25.732
all the different VPCs that
they have in the infrastructure.

00:40:28.827 --> 00:40:31.160
So the two use cases that
we're seeing a lot of traction

00:40:31.160 --> 00:40:33.252
on in the market today.

00:40:33.252 --> 00:40:35.210
The first one is essentially
the cloud on-ramp,

00:40:35.210 --> 00:40:40.910
which is customers who want
to extend their enterprise

00:40:40.910 --> 00:40:44.570
into the cloud, and they
essentially want a protected--

00:40:44.570 --> 00:40:46.700
I call it the protected
first-mile, or last-mile,

00:40:46.700 --> 00:40:48.140
depending on how
you look at it--

00:40:48.140 --> 00:40:52.400
connectivity from the
branch into the cloud.

00:40:52.400 --> 00:40:55.100
And then the second trend
that we have started seeing,

00:40:55.100 --> 00:40:59.450
and we actually are working
very closely with at least three

00:40:59.450 --> 00:41:02.360
or four customers right now
on, is the multi-cloud journey

00:41:02.360 --> 00:41:04.730
where they have different
points of presence

00:41:04.730 --> 00:41:06.560
on different clouds,
and these clouds could

00:41:06.560 --> 00:41:09.020
be public or private.

00:41:09.020 --> 00:41:12.480
So we have one customer
who has data on AWS

00:41:12.480 --> 00:41:15.260
and they run the
service on Google Cloud,

00:41:15.260 --> 00:41:19.330
and they want a high-speed,
multi-cloud connection

00:41:19.330 --> 00:41:22.010
fabric between these two
clouds, and essentially all they

00:41:22.010 --> 00:41:25.910
do is they extend the enterprise
infrastructure into Amazon

00:41:25.910 --> 00:41:27.520
by deploying an ECV there.

00:41:27.520 --> 00:41:29.510
They extend the
infrastructure into Google

00:41:29.510 --> 00:41:31.250
by deploying an ECV there.

00:41:31.250 --> 00:41:33.830
And then Silver
Peak SD-WAN fabric

00:41:33.830 --> 00:41:36.440
thinks of these as new
branches in the cloud

00:41:36.440 --> 00:41:39.890
and does the needful to
make sure that connectivity

00:41:39.890 --> 00:41:40.610
is guaranteed.

00:41:40.610 --> 00:41:46.060
[MUSIC PLAYING]

